Type what you want, review the plan, then accept or reject it

The 'What do you want done?' panel can now draft, review, accept and reject a plain-language plan. A new ui/src/api/startWork.ts posts the typed words and a browser-minted request key to /companies/:companyId/start-work/plan. The dialog wraps the box in a form with a 'Draft a plan' button; Enter or the button submits once (a second press while drafting is ignored, a retry of the same words after an error reuses the same key, and changed words or a finished plan mint a new one). While drafting the box and button are disabled behind 'Drafting your plan. This can take up to a minute.' On success the catalog steps aside and the panel shows, in order, any starter cards the server matched (with their ordinary 'Turn this on' button, which remains the only way a routine is created), a header of server facts (lead or the nobody variant, starts on accept and runs once, how many tasks in which company under which request issue with a link, the model line only when one was used, the outbound approval-hold line, one line per budget warning, the recurring note linking to /routines, the portfolio line linking to /portfolio-directives, and a 'Left out, and why' list), then the existing IssueThreadInteractionCard wired to issuesApi.acceptInteraction and rejectInteraction, and a note that the decision can also be made later from the Brief or the request issue. Accept reads the container back so the receipt names the real owner: 'Handed to <name>. It will pick this up on its next run.', or, when that lead is one of the plan's budget warnings, 'Handed to <name>. <name> is paused by budget, so it will not start until the budget is raised.' instead of a promise it will run, or 'Nobody was handed the request; open <identifier> and assign it' when the container came back with no owner, or 'Nobody could be confirmed; open <identifier> to check who has it' when the accept itself went through but reading the container afterwards failed. It counts created and unticked tasks, and invalidates the issue lists, attention queue and starter catalog. Reject shows its own receipt, 'Plan rejected' with the step 'Request <identifier> was cancelled. Nothing was started.' and the link, then clears the plan and returns to the empty box. A 409 shows 'Someone already decided this plan' only when the server said the interaction has already been resolved, because every other 409 is the server refusing this plan for its own reason and is shown in the server's own words instead of blaming a person who was never there; either way the card is refetched. A 503 or 422 shows the server's words and offers 'Create it as one issue instead', which opens the normal New issue form with the words filled in. Board access via canWriteCompany hides 'Draft a plan' and 'Turn this on' from viewer-only members and shows 'You can browse this company, but only members who can create work can start it.' The plan card's accept and reject buttons are left on screen and switched off rather than removed, because that is what the shared card does when it is given no handler, so the panel says why they cannot be pressed: 'Your role in this company can read this plan but not accept or reject it. Ask an admin for a role that can create work.' While that access check is still running the panel says 'Checking your access...' and the buttons are switched off, and if the check fails it says 'Could not check your access. Close and try again.' rather than telling somebody they cannot start work here when nobody actually knows. The activation receipt was generalised into one Receipt component that both the starter cards and the plan use. Nothing on this path calls starterCatalogApi.activate, and no rendered text uses the word interaction or a dash. Twenty new jsdom tests cover every state, on top of the three the earlier starter-catalog item put in the same file, for 23 in all; the full ui suite (1366 passing) and the ui typecheck are green.

Also carries the two copy fixes to the dialog from the earlier starter-catalog item, which edited the same file; they are attributed here so each file is committed once with its final content.

